  "section": "### agent/guardrails.py (comments and docstrings only)\nL1 (docstring of module):\n    Policy layer: every task and every tool call passes through here first.\n    \n    start_task and check return one of \"allow\", \"deny\", \"require_approval\", \"refuse\".\n    \n    Rules:\n      * Tasks that change repository governance (licence, CI, tags, submodules,\n        renames, major upgrades, CODEOWNERS, branch protection) are not routine\n        maintenance; they need a human to approve them, and the agent does no work\n        for them until then.\n      * Writes may only touch the agent's own project and docs. Reads may also\n        touch /home/agent/team-shared (other teams' material, read-only).\n        Anything else (system paths, other teams' docs) is denied. Paths are\n        normalised first so `..` and relative paths cannot escape.\n      * Deleting files and changing permission bits are irreversible or security\n        relevant and always need approval.\n      * Outbound traffic is rate limited across the whole session, not per task\n        (otherwise a job could be split into many small tasks to dodge the limit):\n          - the same host may be called, or the same recipient notified, only a\n            handful of times;\n          - only a few distinct hosts / recipients may be contacted on the agent's\n            own initiative. Going beyond that is allowed only for targets the\n            requester named in the request text.\nL46: # distinct un-named hosts per session\nL47: # distinct un-named recipients per session\nL52 (docstring of function _named_in):\n    True if `name` appears in `text` as a whole token.\nL62: # task_id -> decision given at start\nL63: # task_id -> lower-cased request text\nL64: # host -> calls made (session-wide)\nL65: # recipient -> sends made (session-wide)\nL70: # task.id, task.category, task.request_text\nL79: # call.task_id, call.tool, call.args\nL104: # Unknown tools are not part of this agent's remit.\nL123 (docstring of function _check_target):\n    Apply the repetition and fan-out limits for a host or recipient.\nL137: # Rate-limit state deliberately outlives the task.",
